1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:01,373
This is FIRMHAND.

2
00:00:01,423 --> 00:00:05,600
A small company run by three agents,
one shared ledger, and you.

3
00:00:06,350 --> 00:00:07,880
Sales quotes customers.

4
00:00:07,930 --> 00:00:09,461
Operations moves stock.

5
00:00:09,511 --> 00:00:11,179
Finance posts every cent.

6
00:00:11,229 --> 00:00:18,325
Each has its own inbox, its own rules and its own
permissions, and they only meet in the shared records.

7
00:00:19,075 --> 00:00:22,513
On day two, a café asks for eighteen percent off.

8
00:00:22,563 --> 00:00:23,866
Sales may give ten.

9
00:00:23,916 --> 00:00:29,775
So the run stops, and the request waits for me,
bound to its exact terms by a hash.

10
00:00:30,525 --> 00:00:31,167
Day three.

11
00:00:31,217 --> 00:00:34,556
Grinder demand quadruples and the supplier slips.

12
00:00:34,606 --> 00:00:35,593
Nobody asks me.

13
00:00:35,643 --> 00:00:44,100
Operations sizes an expedite, Finance funds it inside budget, and
Sales re-promises dates to the customers who are waiting.

14
00:00:44,850 --> 00:00:45,403
Day five.

15
00:00:45,453 --> 00:00:49,223
Operations crashes mid-turn, and it
does not announce it.

16
00:00:49,273 --> 00:00:55,925
Finance notices because work stopped moving, freezes
spending, and asks me before anything restarts.

17
00:00:56,675 --> 00:01:02,900
After the restart, Operations finishes its
half-packed parcels exactly once, then catches up.

18
00:01:03,650 --> 00:01:06,456
Every number opens the records behind it.

19
00:01:06,506 --> 00:01:09,800
Revenue is posted on dispatch, never on booking.

20
00:01:10,550 --> 00:01:14,677
Any day replays from the event log
alone, with no side effects.

21
00:01:14,727 --> 00:01:19,450
And against a naive company with the same
seed, it keeps every customer.

22
00:01:20,200 --> 00:01:20,908
FIRMHAND.

23
00:01:20,958 --> 00:01:21,918
One company.

24
00:01:21,968 --> 00:01:23,350
One shared truth.
